# Line endings for the frontend assets, and why this file exists.
#
# There is no bundler here: hack/copyfrontend copies frontend/index.html,
# frontend/src and frontend/wailsjs verbatim into frontend/dist, and main.go
# embeds that directory with //go:embed. So whatever bytes are in the working
# tree are the bytes that ship, character for character.
#
# That makes line endings load-bearing rather than cosmetic, and it was
# measured rather than reasoned. WebKit's CSS tokenizer turns a CRLF inside a
# multi-line custom-property value into TWO newlines in the computed value, so
# `--font-family-ui` and `--font-family-mono` in src/design/tokens.css read
# back different under `getComputedStyle` from a CRLF checkout than from an LF
# one. The design work hit it by accident while proving that collapsing the
# duplicated dark block onto light-dark() changed no computed value; its
# equivalence evidence would have been wrong had it not noticed.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows as well as Linux, and Git for
# Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true. Without the rules below, a fresh
# clone on Windows gets CRLF in every one of these files and embeds it. The
# repository stores LF today, so this changes nothing for anyone; it stops it
# changing later, silently, on somebody else's machine.
frontend/**            text eol=lf

# The Go toolchain, gofmt and every shell script in hack/ assume LF too, and a
# CRLF shebang line is an execve failure that reads as "no such file".
*.go                   text eol=lf
*.sh                   text eol=lf
*.md                   text eol=lf
go.mod                 text eol=lf
go.sum                 text eol=lf
Makefile               text eol=lf

# Packaging inputs are read by dpkg-deb, gzip and man on a Debian machine.
# packaging/debark-gui.1 in particular is troff: man(7) tolerates CRLF badly
# and lintian notices.
packaging/**           text eol=lf

# Binary, never converted and never diffed as text.
*.png                  binary
*.ico                  binary
